data/evidence/autonomy/session-report-2026-05-14.md:225:4. **Autonomous wishlist-fetch budget** — `HMI_AUTONOMOUS_FETCH=1` enables daily OpenAlex fetch in the daemon. Default-off today. Recommendation: enable, but only on the first tick after midnight UTC, not every tick.
data/evidence/autonomy/system-state-2026-05-28.md:12:A fully autonomous Cochrane-grade library that finds gaps, fetches papers, ingests them, fans them out, synthesizes, and produces a weekly briefing — all without Karen in the loop. Most of the code already exists. The system was broken: 5 papers stuck in the auto-fetched queue, daemon appearing dead.
data/evidence/autonomy/system-state-2026-05-28.md:16:The daemon was firing every 30 min on schedule, but ~90% of ticks today silently no-opped because `git pull --rebase` refused to run with the tree's pending sync-source-counts modifications. Only the 09:30Z tick today actually ran — it successfully fetched 31 new PDFs from OpenAlex into auto-fetched/, ran routing audit, heal-gaps, extract-source-values, and started the manual-fetch ingest before timing out. After that, every subsequent tick failed at the pull step. Morning report stopped on 2026-05-26. Weekly briefing last fired on 2026-05-25.
data/evidence/autonomy/system-state-2026-05-28.md:19:- The `auto-fetched/` folder where the wishlist fetcher drops new papers was deliberately excluded from Claude's ingest path (cost guard — Codex was supposed to drain it). Codex's external scheduler had a stop condition of "≤10 PDFs" which fired immediately whenever the queue was small, leaving the residual indefinitely.
data/evidence/autonomy/system-state-2026-05-28.md:27:2. **`tools/autonomy/ingest-manual-fetch-batch.mjs`** — added `raw/manual-fetch/seasonal-geographic-variance/auto-fetched/` as a 5th MANUAL_FETCH_ROOTS entry with priority FIRST and `AUTO_FETCHED_PER_TICK_CAP=3` so the Opus path stays cost-bounded. Closes the gap left by Codex's "≤10 PDFs" stop threshold.
data/evidence/autonomy/system-state-2026-05-28.md:35:I triggered the manual-fetch ingest via the same code path the daemon uses (`node tools/autonomy/ingest-manual-fetch-batch.mjs --max-pdfs=1`). The nested `claude --print` subprocess spawned successfully, read the PDF, ran identity checks against `wiki/sources/`, found that the auto-fetched PDF was byte-identical to an already-ingested page (`kim2018-dry-dog-food-heavy-metals`) under a misleading filename, and STOPped cleanly with that diagnosis. The tracker was updated; no false-positive ingest; no duplicate page created. End-to-end dedup path works.
