data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-stops-2026-05-30.md:40:**Discover-skill mis-score pattern (same as `camel2025-camel-milk-composition-5` above):** The filename `camel2026-camel-milk-composition-1.pdf` was auto-fetched as part of the same "camel milk composition" topic sweep that pulled the legitimate heavy-metals papers in this folder (`afzal2024-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `chen2020-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `hasan2021-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `konuspayeva2010-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `massoud2020-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `mirmahdi2021-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`, `akhmetsadykova2012-camel-milk-heavy-metals.pdf`). Two stops in one day for `*-camel-milk-composition-*` filenames from the same Manual Fetch Discovery sweep confirms the scoring-rule gap is reproducible: the discover skill's topic-fit gate accepts "composition" as a heavy-metals proxy when it should require an explicit heavy-metals analyte token (Pb/Cd/As/Hg/Ni/Al/Cr/Sn/Sb/U or "heavy metals" / "trace metals" / "toxic metals" / "metal contamination") in title/abstract/keywords. The other four remaining `*-camel-milk-composition-*` PDFs in this folder (`peptidomic2026-camel-milk-composition-4.pdf`, `potential2024-camel-milk-composition-8.pdf`, `processing2026-camel-milk-composition-2.pdf`, `ultrasound2025-camel-milk-composition-6.pdf`) are likely to follow the same pattern and should be triaged before further auto-fetches into this folder.
