data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-stops-2026-05-30.md:142:**Discover-skill mis-score pattern (auto-fetched/ folder, distinct from the camel-milk Discovery sweep):** The filename `auto-fish-freshwater-tas-product_2023_*` was auto-fetched against a `freshwater fish × tAs × product` gap-fill query. The PDF's title ("Rising stars in comparative and clinical medicine") and abstract (which keywords as "livestock, poultry, microbiota, drug resistance, probiotics, toxicity") contain none of the gap-fill query terms; the auto-fetcher matched on body-text mentions of "freshwater fish rohu," "arsenic and chromium," and "fish ponds" inside three secondhand-summary paragraphs. This is the same root cause pattern as the camel-milk-composition stops earlier today: the topic-fit gate accepts body-text proximity matches when it should require the heavy-metals analyte token AND the matrix token to co-occur in title / abstract / keywords, AND should down-weight document type = `Editorial` / `Research Topic Introduction` / `Commentary` regardless. An editorial's body will mention any number of the constituent papers' topics secondhand without itself being primary research.
