data/workbench/standards-book/parts/04-the-standards/00a-methodological-disclosure.md:19:Verdicts (PROMOTE, REVISE, QUARANTINE) and all applied corrections are logged to `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` and to per-page commit messages in the project's git history. The dual-instrument architecture is conceptually similar to the dual-reviewer protocol used in Cochrane systematic reviews [41]; the substitution of two AI instruments for two human reviewers is documented openly rather than concealed, and every wiki claim traces through git history to the source citation and the audit verdict.
data/workbench/standards-book/parts/04-the-standards/00a-methodological-disclosure.md:25:Where either instrument surfaces a finding requiring human judgment (a paper-internal contradiction, a true duplicate conflict, a new product-category proposal, an HMTc-relevant policy decision), the instrument stops and surfaces to the principal investigator rather than guessing. This boundary is enforced operationally (stop conditions in the operating manuals), logged transparently (per-session run summaries in `data/evidence/codex-runs/` and morning reports in `data/evidence/autonomy/`), and reproducible from git history.
data/workbench/standards-book/build/HMTc-Program-Manual-2026-v0.md:1693:Verdicts (PROMOTE, REVISE, QUARANTINE) and all applied corrections are logged to `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` and to per-page commit messages in the project's git history. The dual-instrument architecture is conceptually similar to the dual-reviewer protocol used in Cochrane systematic reviews [41]; the substitution of two AI instruments for two human reviewers is documented openly rather than concealed, and every wiki claim traces through git history to the source citation and the audit verdict.
data/workbench/standards-book/build/HMTc-Program-Manual-2026-v0.md:1699:Where either instrument surfaces a finding requiring human judgment (a paper-internal contradiction, a true duplicate conflict, a new product-category proposal, an HMTc-relevant policy decision), the instrument stops and surfaces to the principal investigator rather than guessing. This boundary is enforced operationally (stop conditions in the operating manuals), logged transparently (per-session run summaries in `data/evidence/codex-runs/` and morning reports in `data/evidence/autonomy/`), and reproducible from git history.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:20:- **Manual-fetch (MFK/MR/PCMF) ingest.** The 335 hand-curated PDFs in `raw/Manual Fetch Kimi /`, `raw/Manual Resourcing/`, `raw/Papers Cube Manual Fetch/`. Runs via the autonomy daemon (`tools/autonomy/daemon-tick.sh` → `tools/autonomy/ingest-manual-fetch-batch.mjs`). Every 30 minutes, picks up to 2 unprocessed PDFs, runs the full Cochrane workflow (read PDF + identity-check + write fresh OR merge-enhance + spawn fresh-context audit subagent + apply audit findings + commit). Per-PDF state tracked at `data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-tracker.csv`.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:30:**Role A — Manual-fetch ingest (parallel to Claude's daemon).** Codex processes manual-fetch PDFs from a different folder set than the daemon, doubling throughput. Same Cochrane workflow as the daemon (`tools/autonomy/ingest-manual-fetch-batch.mjs` reference): read PDF, identity-check against `wiki/sources/`, write fresh OR merge-enhance, run routing audit, spawn a fresh-context audit subagent via Codex's own Agent equivalent, apply audit findings independently, commit per PDF, append to `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` with status=pending (Claude will cross-audit later).
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:46:- **Convergence point with daemon:** daemon is still loaded in launchd and ticking. The per-PDF tracker (`data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-tracker.csv`) prevents race conditions — either agent that claims a PDF first wins; the other skips. Daemon will simply exit cleanly each tick after finding nothing un-tracked in its priority order, which is fine. **Papers Cube Manual Fetch is now FULLY done as of 2026-05-18 (Claude completed the rotation); Children Personal Care Papers is also fully done.** No active claim conflicts with Claude Code; both those folders should be skipped.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:48:**Role B — Cross-vendor audits.** Codex is the second set of AI eyes on every wiki source page produced by Claude OR by Codex itself (the daemon's audit subagent is a Claude subagent; Codex's audit subagent is a Codex subagent; the cross-vendor pass on the audit-queue gives EVERY page one Claude audit + one GPT-5.5 audit). The "fresh context" requirement (Part 2 firewall, brand firewall, slug vocabulary, speciation, numerical fidelity) is best satisfied by a different model family, not just a different same-model subagent.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:50:Codex's audit role draws from `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` (the producer-consumer queue both agents populate after each ingest). Per audit work cycle:
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:53:2. Read `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv`. Pick the next row with `status=pending` or `status=pending-brand-firewall-recheck-locked-strict`, oldest first.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:83:10. Update `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` row: `status` → `audited-codex-promote` / `audited-codex-revised` / `audited-codex-quarantine`; `audit_verdict` → the verdict; `audit_applied_at` → today's ISO date; `notes` → append "; Codex: <N> findings, <X> applied, <Y> rejected."
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:129:1. **DRAIN-AUDIT-QUEUE goal**: reduce `status=pending` and `status=pending-brand-firewall-recheck-locked-strict` rows in `data/evidence/audit-queue.csv` to zero, OR to a named target count (e.g., "drain to ≤20 pending").
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:130:2. **DRAIN-FOLDER goal**: every PDF in a named folder ends the session either represented by a `wiki/sources/*.md` page (real ingest), folded into an existing source page (same-DOI rollup), or marked in `data/evidence/wishlist-fetch-audit.csv` with an explicit `skip:*` / `fail:*` / `ingested` decision. Folder ends empty of un-dispositioned PDFs.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:135:The session prompt assigns one goal as primary. If the primary goal is met before the session naturally ends, Codex switches to the next-priority goal type (audit-queue → drain-folder → prose-sections → lint → marker-drafts) and continues until ANY of: (a) the new goal is also met, (b) a hard blocker fires, or (c) the session is interrupted externally.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:144:4. **TRIAGE-FIRST FOR DRAIN-FOLDER GOALS.** Auto-fetched wishlist folders are heavily padded with same-DOI duplicates, false-positives, and download failures. The drain-folder goal is met fastest by: (a) opening every PDF in the folder in parallel batches of 10, (b) classifying each as `real-ingest` / `dedup-rollup` / `skip:<reason>` / `blocker`, (c) batch-committing all `dedup-rollup` and `skip:*` decisions to `wishlist-fetch-audit.csv` in one or two commits, (d) then doing real ingests on the remaining `real-ingest` pile. Interleaving triage with full ingest per PDF is slower.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:169:The full backlog (manual-fetch PDFs + strict-recheck rows + Marker corpus + auto-fetched wishlist accumulations) is NOT a single-session goal. Multiple sessions are expected. Each session's job is to meet its named goal, no more, no less.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:173:- Do NOT ingest manual-fetch PDFs from folders assigned to Claude's daemon (06_Infant_Foods_Formula, Children Personal Care Papers, 04_Meat_Poultry_Seafood, 03_Grains_and_Grain_Products, 05_Dairy_Eggs_and_Alternatives). The daemon owns these. Codex's ingest assignment is the bottom-up priority list (condiments, nuts/seeds, processed, vegetables, fruits, Papers Cube, Manual Resourcing). Before ingesting any PDF, check `data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-tracker.csv` — if the PDF is already tracked, skip it (the daemon claimed it).
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:188:- If a true conflict happens (both editing same wiki source page in same hour), git will show it; the second commit wins on the conflicted lines. The audit-queue.csv tracks the audit application separately, so the verdict is preserved even if the wiki page reverts.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:190:If Codex sees a wiki source page mid-edit (e.g., daemon is currently ingesting it), the audit-queue.csv `status` will still be `pending` for that cite-key — the daemon writes the row only after its own audit completes. So queue-driven picking automatically avoids in-flight work.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:199:GOAL: data/evidence/audit-queue.csv has 0 rows with status=pending or status=pending-brand-firewall-recheck-locked-strict. Stop when met OR when a hard blocker fires (three consecutive QUARANTINE verdicts, paper-internal contradiction, DOI conflict requiring Karen).
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:203:Output: data/evidence/codex-runs/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<HHMM>.md with goal-met state (queue depth before/after), per-page verdicts, commit hashes, hard-blocker name if stopped early.
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:208:### Template B — DRAIN-FOLDER (auto-fetched wishlist or manual-fetch)
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:211:GOAL: <FOLDER_PATH> has 0 un-dispositioned PDFs. Every PDF ends either represented by a wiki/sources/*.md page (real ingest), folded into an existing page as a same-DOI rollup (with the wishlist row marked ingested), or marked in data/evidence/wishlist-fetch-audit.csv with an explicit skip:<reason> or fail:<reason> decision. Stop when met OR hard blocker (paper-internal contradiction, DOI conflict).
docs/codex-collaboration/README.md:213:How: TRIAGE FIRST. Open every PDF in the folder in parallel batches of 10. Classify each as (a) real-ingest, (b) dedup-rollup, (c) skip:<reason>, (d) blocker. Batch-commit all (b)+(c) decisions to wishlist-fetch-audit.csv in one or two commits. Then real-ingest the (a) pile, batch-committing every ~10 source pages. Per CLAUDE.md Part 4, do NOT commit per-paper.
