wiki/methodology/search-strategy.md:11:This page is the Cochrane-equivalent search-protocol publication for the Heavy Metal Index. It documents which academic and grey-literature databases are queried, how candidate papers are deduped against the existing corpus, the scoring rubric that determines whether a paper is auto-fetched for ingest, and the inclusion / exclusion criteria. A reader auditing the defensibility of the Index — regulator, brand-legal expert, journalist, or competing standards body — should be able to land here and replicate the search.
wiki/methodology/search-strategy.md:46:Every candidate paper from every database is checked against the existing corpus before being scored or fetched. The dedupe index is rebuilt from `wiki/sources/` and `data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-tracker.csv` at the start of every discovery run (`tools/discovery/build-corpus-index.mjs`).
wiki/briefings/weekly-2026-05-20.md:424:1. **Build `tools/audit-corpus-saturation.mjs` that computes per-cell coverage ratios against OpenAlex.** For each (ingredient × metal) pair, query OpenAlex for the canonical search string (`"<ingredient>" AND ("<metal>" OR "<symbol>")`), record the result count, and write `data/evidence/corpus-saturation.csv` with columns `ingredient_slug, metal, wiki_sources, openalex_total, saturation_pct, top_missing_dois`. Gate the routing-audit's `READY` status on saturation ≥ 60%; currently-`READY` cells with low saturation get demoted to `PROVISIONAL` until the gap is closed. The autonomy loop's next-ingest priority becomes "lowest saturation × highest HMTc importance," which is what `data/evidence/autonomy/manual-fetch-tracker.csv` should be sorted on.
