Results And Discussion
3.1 Valuation and Comparison of Pb in Branded and Nonbranded Spices:
The digested and entirely prepared samples (including blank, duplicate and spike samples)
were analysed using a flame atomic absorption spectrophotometer, and the concentration of
Pb was calculated using equation no. (1) at the mg/kg level on a weight-weight basis. The measured
concentration of Pb for branded and nonbranded spices is presented in Table 2 with the standard
deviation. The average concentration of Pb, data range, and maximum allowable limit are also mentioned
in the Table 2.
The average value of the branded spices decreased in the order turmeric powder> coriander>cumin> red
pepper chili. However, the nonbranded items showed the order of turmeric>cumin powder and red chili>
coriander. This implies that the turmeric powder was most adulterated as branded and nonbranded items.
The study also found that the average value of Pb for Cumin Powder, Red Pepper powder and Turmeric
powder were higher for nonbranded spice than branded items except Coriander. Here one of the most
important point have to notice that some of the value of turmeric powder was unexpectedly too high than
other in both the category.

