Integrating GWR with finer-scale geospatial data could clarify mechanisms driving
these patterns, such as whether Fe’s dominance in Dejen reflects unique basaltic or granitic soil geology or anthropogenic inputs distinct from neighboring regions. Besides,
several source apportionment methods including positive matrix factorization, principal
component analysis, chemical mass balance, isotope ratio analysis and enrichment factors can be widely used to identify and quantify the contributions of different contamination sources to heavy metal concentrations in environmental samples. These methods
are critical for designing targeted mitigation strategies, especially in areas like Dejen,
Gozamen, and Sinan, where spatial variations in metal levels suggest diverse sources.
3.4 Environmental and health implications

The spatial variability underscores the role of geogenic and anthropogenic factors in
metal uptake. For instance, Sinan’s higher Fe and Zn align with its lateritic and granitic
soil compositions, while Dejen’s Pb highlights legacy contamination from automobile
fuels [36].
