For all metals, recoveries fell within the internationally accepted range of 80–120% for
trace metal analysis [24], confirming the method’s accuracy. In Dejen and Gozamen,
recoveries for Fe (95.8–96.9%), Cu (94.8–98.8%), Zn (96.4–97%), Pb (94.8–95.2%), and
Cd (95.3–96.4%) demonstrated exceptional agreement with expected values, indicating
minimal matrix interference. The Sinan area showed marginally higher recoveries for Cu
(102.8%), Zn (108%), and Cd (104.2%), likely due to elevated background metal levels
(interferences) or incomplete matrix digestion, a known challenge in plant analysis [25].
Precision, measured by RSD, was generally ≤ 10% for most metals (e.g., Dejen: Cu RSD
= 2.4%; Gozamen: Zn RSD = 1%), aligning with the International Council for Harmonization (ICH) guideline for bioanalytical method validation (ICH Q2(R1), which mandates RSD < 15% for precision [26]. Exceptions included Pb in Gozamen (RSD = 11.1%)
and Cd in Sinan (RSD = 11.1%), which may reflect inhomogeneous sample distribution
or instrumental noise at near-limit detection levels. Notably, Cu in Sinan (RSD = 9.13%)
and Zn in Dejen (RSD = 6%) still met validation criteria for environmental samples,
where higher variability is tolerated [27].
The consistency of recoveries across regions such as Fe: 95.8–99.6%, Cu: 98.8–102%,
Zn: 96.4–108%, Pb: 94.8–99% and Cd: 95.3–101.4% underscore the method’s robustness
despite geographical variations in plant composition. These results validate the procedure’s reliability for quantifying heavy metals in medicinal plants, as recovery and precision metrics comply with WHO guidelines for herbal product safety assessment [28].
3.3 Heavy metal concentrations in Nigella sativa across three geographical areas

The concentrations of heavy metals in Nigella sativa samples from Dejen, Gozamen,
and Sinan revealed distinct distribution patterns, reflecting variations in environmental

