exposure and anthropogenic influences (Table 4). Iron (Fe) levels were highest in Dejen
(82.90 ± 0.7 mg/kg), followed by Gozamen (71.48 ± 1.89 mg/kg) and Sinan (71.38 ± 1.34
mg/kg), likely due to naturally iron-rich soils in Dejen, as lateritic and granitic parent
materials often elevate Fe content in plants [29]. While Fe is essential, excessive accumulation in medicinal plants may impair their therapeutic efficacy [30].
Copper (Cu) concentrations ranged from 9.18 ± 0.22 to 18.24 ± 2.03 mg/kg (Sinan),
with Sinan’s higher values potentially linked to agricultural use of Cu-based fungicides, a
common practice in intensive farming regions [31]. All areas, however, remained below
the WHO permissible limit of 20 mg/kg for Cu in herbal materials [28]. Zinc (Zn) levels
followed a similar trend, peaking in Sinan (71.25 ± 2.79 mg/kg) and Gozamen (71.15 ±
0.08 mg/kg) followed by the lowest level in Dejen area (55.27 ± 0.33 mg/kg). Elevated Zn
in Sinan and Gozamen may stem from long term use of phosphate fertilizers, land exposure to heavy car traffic and inadequate irrigation practices [32].
Lead (Pb) and cadmium, both non-essential toxic metals, showed concerning disparities. Pb concentrations were highest in Dejen (0.21 ± 0.1 mg/kg), followed by Gozamen
(0.18 ± 0.02 mg/kg) slightly lower than the WHO threshold of 10 mg/kg comparable to
stricter guidelines for daily consumption of 0.5 µg/kg bw/day [33]. This likely reflects
Dejen’s proximity to vehicular traffic, as Pb residues from historical leaded gasoline persist in soils. Conversely, Cd levels were higher in Gozamen (0.12 ± 0.004 mg/kg), far less
than the WHO limit of 1.5 mg/kg [34]. Sinan’s lower Pb (0.11 ± 0.05 mg/kg) and Cd (0.09
± 0.007 mg/kg) levels suggest lesser industrial influence.
The pronounced spatial heterogeneity in metal concentrations, such as Fe peaking in
Dejen (82.90 ± 0.7) but remaining lower in Gozamen (71.48 ± 1.89) and Sinan (71.38
± 1.34), or Cd surging in Gozamen (1.2 ± 0.04) compared to adjacent areas, could be
attributed to environmental or anthropogenic factors such as soil composition, industrial activity, or agricultural practices influence metal levels differently across regions.
