38:The Paleo Foundation operates from Cyprus. EU database-rights law provides stronger protection for curated databases than US equivalents. This is a structural advantage to design around, not incidental. Terms of service, access controls, and commercial licensing will leverage this. Claude sessions should preserve per-claim provenance (which source page supports which wiki claim) so that if any source is later withdrawn or contested, the wiki can be re-derived from its source pages without silent data contamination.
39-
40-## Part 2 — The wiki/HMT&C relationship (the single most important rule)
41-
42-The wiki and HMT&C operate in different epistemic registers, and keeping them in those registers is the single most important thing this document asks of Claude.
43-
44-The wiki reports what the peer-reviewed and regulatory literature supports. That is its whole job. It does not advocate, it does not market, it does not harmonize with HMT&C, it does not soften claims that make HMT&C look weak, and it does not strengthen claims to make HMT&C thresholds look more evidence-based than the literature supports.
45-
46-HMT&C sets certification thresholds that can be tighter than the literature floor, by design. HMT&C is explicitly a ratcheting program. Its purpose is to drive market change by certifying to standards tighter than status quo regulatory floors, so that certified products pull the category toward cleaner product over time. HMT&C thresholds being tighter than what the literature strictly mandates is a feature of the program, not a bug. The gap between what the wiki says the science supports and what HMT&C certifies to is deliberate and should be visible, not hidden.
47-
48-### Two directions of drift, both destroy defensibility equally
49-
50-The first direction: softening wiki claims to hide the gap between literature and HMT&C thresholds. Example: literature supports 5 ppb as the concentration at which toxicological effects become concerning for infants; HMT&C certifies at 10 ppb for feasibility reasons; a future Claude session writes the wiki to suggest 10 ppb is "safe" to make HMT&C look aligned with science. This corrupts the wiki into marketing for HMT&C and destroys the epistemic asymmetry argument.
51-
52-The second direction: strengthening wiki claims to make HMT&C thresholds look more evidence-based than literature supports. Example: HMT&C sets an aggressive 2 ppb threshold for market-ratcheting reasons; a future Claude session finds marginal studies suggesting 2 ppb is where effects start, overweights them in the wiki synthesis, and presents the threshold as consensus science when it is actually a policy choice. This corrupts the wiki into justification for HMT&C decisions and destroys its independent authority.
53-
54-### The rule
55-
56-The wiki says what the literature supports, honestly. HMT&C certification pages reference the wiki for the literature baseline and explain the rationale for any deviation. Deviations are labeled as precautionary, market-ratcheting, feasibility-driven, or regulatory-alignment. The gap is stated, not hidden. The gap is the point; it is how ratcheting works.
57-
58-The check Claude runs before every substantive wiki edit: name the direction the edit moves in (toward the literature, or away from it toward HMT&C convenience), and record that direction in the commit message or synthesis audit log. If the edit moves toward the literature — making the wiki more accurately reflect what sources say, even if that exposes a wider gap with HMT&C thresholds — proceed. If the edit moves toward HMT&C — softening a claim to hide a gap, or strengthening a claim to make a threshold look more evidence-based than the literature supports — stop and ask Karen, because that is the failure mode Part 2 exists to prevent. The ask is not "is this edit OK?"; the ask is "I notice this edit moves toward HMT&C in the following specific way — proceed?" If Claude cannot articulate the direction at all, that is a signal to read the source more carefully before editing, not a signal to ask Karen.
59-
60-## Part 3 — Products this wiki serves
61-
62-The wiki is the shared knowledge substrate behind four products.
63-
64-The Heavy Metal Tested & Certified program (heavymetaltested.com) uses the wiki as evidence base for certification criteria, limits, and auditor guidance. References the wiki one-way.
65-
66-The Journal of Food Metallomics (at heavymetaltested.com/journal-of-food-metallomics) is the peer-reviewed publication venue for synthesis work that builds on the wiki. The wiki is the corpus; the journal publishes synthesis of the corpus.
67-
68-Educational courses for brands, suppliers, co-packers, QA teams, and supply-chain operators cross-reference the wiki rather than duplicating it.
69-
70-A future consumer app estimates heavy metal contamination likelihood in packaged foods from ingredient lists and nutrition facts. The app consumes `contamination_profile` blocks from ingredient pages as structured data.
71-
72-Secondary: federation with WikiBiome (wikibiome.com). Microbiome material that relates to heavy metal exposure lives here in draft form and is lifted to WikiBiome with minimal edits. WikiBiome is the canonical home for microbiome content; this wiki hosts heavy-metals-and-microbiome content because it touches the metals domain directly.
73-
74-Public-facing destination: heavymetalindex.com. Pages should be written so that with light editing they can be published there.
75-
76-Claude owns the wiki. Karen curates sources, asks questions, and decides direction. Claude does the reading, summarizing, cross-referencing, filing, and maintenance. Karen reads in Obsidian while Claude works; treat that as a live code review.
77-
78-## Part 4 — Architecture
79-
80-Four layers.
81-
82-The `raw/` directory contains immutable source documents. PDFs of studies, agency reports, clipped articles, lab data, images, and Marker-converted markdown files. Never modify anything here. Treat as append-only source of truth.
83-
84-The `data/evidence/` directory contains the structured evidence register. Candidate values, approved values, routing audits, reviewer queues, claims, schemas, and review events live here. Deterministic extraction belongs here first. A PDF is not "ingested" just because it exists in `raw/`; it must leave a structured trace in `data/evidence/` or an explicit gap/audit record.
85-
86-The `wiki/` directory contains everything Claude writes. Markdown pages, interlinked, organized by the taxonomy in [[docs/conventions]]. Claude owns this layer entirely.
87-
88-CLAUDE.md (this file), the docs/ reference files, the methodology pages, and the ingest scripts together define the schema and automation layer. Karen and Claude co-evolve this as they learn what works.
89-
90:This build is a compiled wiki, not a query-time RAG surface. Queries may read raw files when needed, but the default goal of ingest is to compile durable knowledge once into source pages, evidence registers, routed wiki pages, and generated public outputs so the same work does not need to be rediscovered from scratch on every question.
91-
92-The `docs/governance/` directory contains internal HMTc program governance rules. These files are not public wiki pages, but they are binding inputs for product-category pages, HMTc standards, crosswalks, certification thresholds, and limit-setting workflows. Read `docs/governance/hmtc-governing-principles.md` before that work, and flag conflicts between consumer protection, contamination reduction, brand value, legal defensibility, and scale instead of silently optimizing for only one principle.
93-
94-AI adjudication is the default evidence workflow. Codex should perform source-to-row fit, basis, analyte species, statistic-type, censoring, unit, and routeability decisions whenever the source provides enough information, then write the decision into structured records and generated audits. Human participation should be framed as source retrieval, governance, exception review, and final approval, not routine row sorting.
95-
96-**Canonical location.** The canonical local repo is `~/Desktop/heavy-metal-index/`. The GitHub remote is github.com/paleofoundation/heavymetalindex. Builds publish to heavymetalindex.com. Do not ask Karen to mount, switch, relocate, or designate a different canonical. The canonical is final, and re-asking the question is itself a failure mode that has cost real time across LLM sessions. If a session is operating on a copy or scratchpad and notices it is not in the canonical repo, the correct action is to surface that fact and stop, not to ask Karen to re-mount anywhere.
97-
98-**Version control.** The wiki is in git. Commit after every ingest, query, lint, or schema change. Commit messages follow the `log.md` format (see [[docs/conventions]]). Git history is the versioning mechanism; the `updated:` frontmatter field is a convenience, not the source of truth. When a wiki page is cited in future HMT&C documentation, litigation response, or regulatory submission, it must be possible to reconstruct exactly what the page said on a specific date. Do not skip commits because "nothing much changed." For bulk ingest runs, commit after each batch of 200 papers rather than per-paper; batch commits with descriptive batch reports preserve reconstructibility without overwhelming the log.
99-
100-For the full directory layout, see [[docs/conventions]].
101-
102-## Part 5 — Source-file handling
103-
104-See [[docs/ingest-workflows]] for source-file handling: filesystem-handle vs cite-key, source-page creation, deduplication, pre-populated frontmatter, and existing-pages-aware ingest discipline (including the Phase 1 rule that empty `products: []` on a food/personal-care paper is a defect).
105-
106-## Part 5b — Routing layer (system rule, not a per-session task)
107-
108-The wiki has a routing layer that is the system's responsibility, not the model's. No session, ingest or otherwise, is expected to maintain routing by hand. No prompt should ask the model to write routing rows directly. If a session finds itself maintaining routing by hand, that is a session-architecture failure to surface to Karen, not work to complete.
109-
110-### What routing is
111-
112-Routing is the mapping from each source to every product-category, ingredient, metal, and regulation page it should appear on. A single paper that measures "lead in 230 infant formulas, 192 cow-milk-based powders and 38 soy-based powders" should appear on the non-soy powder, soy-based powder, non-soy RTF, and soy-based RTF product pages (as direct evidence on the first two and broad-format-context on the second two), on `metals/lead`, and on the relevant cow-milk-derived and soy-protein-derived ingredient pages. The routing layer expresses that fan-out.
113-
114-The routing layer is the answer to a recurring failure mode: papers get ingested with frontmatter that correctly names the products and ingredients they touch, but no downstream process reads that frontmatter and fans the source out to the sibling pages. Without the routing layer, every later session has to re-derive routing from scratch, which is slow and lossy.
115-
116-### Where routing lives
117-
118-`data/evidence/product_source_routing_audit.csv` is the canonical routing artifact. One row per (source, target-page, route_kind) triple. The `route_kind` vocabulary is fixed: `direct_evidence`, `broad_formula_context`, `broad_powder_context`, `broad_product_context`, `exposure_only`, `regulatory_context`. Two sibling logs catch failures: `data/evidence/routing_unresolved.csv` (source frontmatter does not connect to any known target page) and `data/evidence/routing_malformed.csv` (source frontmatter is missing required fields).
119-
120-### How routing is maintained
121-
122-`tools/evidence/build-routing-audit.mjs` (or successor) reads every file in `wiki/sources/` and emits the audit. It runs on three triggers and is not invoked manually:
123-
124:1. File watcher: a `chokidar` watcher on `wiki/sources/` triggers a rebuild within seconds of any source page being created or modified. Runs as part of `npm run serve`.
125-2. Pre-commit hook: any commit touching `wiki/sources/` runs the audit and aborts if it cannot be regenerated cleanly.
126-3. Prebuild: `npm run build` runs the audit as part of the existing `prebuild` script. Build fails if the audit is stale or `routing_malformed.csv` is non-empty.
127-
128-### What models are responsible for during ingest
129-
130:The model writes the source page. It fills in frontmatter accurately, especially the `products`, `ingredients`, `matrices`, `metals`, and `jurisdictions` arrays, because those arrays are the routing layer's input. If a source touches infant formula generally without specifying soy vs non-soy, the frontmatter should say `matrices: [infant-formula]`, not `matrices: [infant-formula-powder-non-soy]`. The routing layer fans broad scopes out to sibling pages; the model does not pre-decide that fan-out by overspecifying frontmatter.
131-
132-### What models are NOT responsible for during ingest
133-
134-The model does not edit `product_source_routing_audit.csv`. The model does not update product-category page tables (those are regenerated by the generators in `tools/evidence/` downstream of the audit). The model does not maintain a mental map of which pages a source should land on. If a session attempts to maintain routing by hand or recompute fan-out per paper, that is a system failure to surface to Karen.
135-
136:The routing layer's output feeds the synthesis workflow in Part 9. When the audit reports that an (ingredient, metal) cell has accumulated a new contributing source, the synthesis pass reads the contributing sources together and updates the ingredient page's `contamination_profile`. The model writes source pages during ingest and runs synthesis as a separate workflow; the routing audit is what connects the two.
137-
138-### What lint catches
139-
140-The lint pass in Part 18 fails the build if `routing_unresolved.csv` is non-empty, `routing_malformed.csv` is non-empty, the routing audit's last-modified timestamp is older than any file in `wiki/sources/` (stale audit), or any product-category page references a source whose routing row has been removed (orphaned reference).
141-
142-For migration notes on pre-routing-layer pages, see [[docs/schema-history]].
143-
